budget ledger records this under the `action:clean_filter` label. This is the same mechanism used by SiphonDetritus and RemoveShrimp, neither of which returns an explicit `BudgetDelta`.\n\n3. **Consistent exemption pattern**: The budget guard (`tick_has_closed_system_nitrogen`) correctly exempts all six open-system actions (feed, water_change, siphon_detritus, clean_filter, add_shrimp, remove_shrimp). CleanFilter follows exactly the same pattern as SiphonDetritus and RemoveShrimp — implicit export via snapshot diff, no explicit budget delta, exempted from conservation checks.\n\nRouting the removed biomass back into detritus or dissolved organics would be semantically wrong — it would keep mass inside the tank that is physically being removed during filter cleaning. The amendment's alternative suggestion (document + explicit budget entry) is largely already satisfied by ROUTING.md documentation and the existing snapshot-diff tracking. No code change is warranted."}],"summary":"The sole disputed amendment (fr-4-00ebb97d) is rejected. CleanFilter's biomass reduction is an intentional open-system maintenance export, documented in ROUTING.md, tracked via before/after snapshot diffs in the budget ledger, and correctly exempted from closed-system nitrogen validation — following the same pattern as SiphonDetritus and RemoveShrimp. Routing removed biomass back into in-tank pools would be semantically incorrect, and the documentation/audit concerns raised by the amendment are already substantially addressed by the existing code and docs."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"03aa46b8-c4f6-4667-9c5b-114f8e7a451a"}
